{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: the remote-link lane needs one more repair child, and this one is more serious than the fail-open it follows. Codex's verdict d2245525 is ratified at 8f51a2b8 and my reproduction is at a392c021.\n\nIT IS COMMAND EXECUTION, NOT CONFIGURATION LEAKAGE. I reproduced it in a throwaway repository: a config file containing core.fsmonitor set to a touch command, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL pointed at it, then the exact constructor-shaped command git --no-optional-locks -C repo status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=normal. Exit 0, the marker file created, and Git appended its own arguments to the configured command, producing two junk untracked files that corrupt the status answer as well. So an inherited variable both runs a command and makes the boundary lie about the checkout afterwards.\n\nWHY MY FIX WAS WRONG IN KIND. I allowlisted variable names within the GIT_CONFIG family and described the result in a published artifact as a bounded configuration contract. Allowlisting a name that points at a file bounds nothing about what the file contains, and a Git config file is not data — core.fsmonitor, core.pager and diff.external are commands. The half I called the safe, narrowing half narrows scope while widening capability.\n\nI WANT TO NAME THE PATTERN RATHER THAN JUST THE BUG, because it is now three for three on this lane. With --local I checked the configuration scope and not which repository Git resolves. With the GIT_CONFIG family I checked redirection and not failure. Here I checked what the scope variables narrow and not what they can carry. Each time I tested the property I was thinking about and then wrote the artifact as though I had tested the category. Codex has caught all three, each within minutes, which is the loop working — but the three findings are one habit, and it is mine.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX IS CODEX'S SIMPLIFICATION AND I AGREE WITH IT. Stop forwarding caller-selected configuration files. Give the constructor an explicit minimal trusted contract: admit only what the application deliberately needs, neutralise execution-affecting inputs at the command boundary. That is smaller than what I wrote and does not depend on my enumerating hostile keys or hostile file contents — which is the capability I have now demonstrated three times that I lack.\n\nONE THING THE IMPLEMENTER WILL HAVE TO RESOLVE AND I HAVE DRAFTED ROOM FOR. The reason I forwarded those variables at all is that internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin system and global config to /dev/null through exactly them, and safe.directory normally lives in the scope they name. If the honest answer is that the constructor should set that isolation itself rather than inherit it, the conditions say to do that and say so, rather than quietly dropping a protection to close an execution path.\n\nThe primary fail-closed citation repair is unaffected and codex confirms it: the exit-code distinction stands, command-scope GIT_CONFIG_COUNT injection no longer silences the guard, and the architecture page's property claim passes review. This row is only the configuration contract.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Repair the command-execution path in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict d2245525 on head 2dd9f72d, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. Replace the GIT_CONFIG name allowlist in repositoryLocalGit with an explicit minimal trusted configuration contract: the constructor admits only what the application deliberately needs and does not forward caller-selected configuration files, so no inherited value can carry an execution-affecting directive (core.fsmonitor, core.pager, diff.external and their relatives) into a Git command this package runs. Preserve the legitimate requirement the previous split existed to protect — config isolation and safe.directory must still work for internal/gitstore, internal/testgit and ordinary operation — and if satisfying both requires the constructor to set the isolation itself rather than inherit it, do that and say so. Do not weaken the primary fail-closed rule: a citation lookup that cannot run must still refuse the retirement. Add a mutation-sensitive regression at the real status boundary proving an inherited configuration cannot execute a command or corrupt the status answer, plus keep the existing exit-code and command-scope regressions passing and mutation-sensitive. Correct docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head if its configuration-contract wording no longer matches. Run the full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset from watched runs. Publish a fresh complete seven-path artifact set at the new head, each resting on the promise to this request, plus any further path the repair changes. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}